NEXT: BEST GUESS -- one candidate, and it is a candidate, not a recommendation.

THE MECHANISM IS MEASURED, THE REPAIR IS NOT RULED. setParse builds each of its
two decoration attributes in this order:

    GroupItem *builtinParsE = field->addString("builtinParsE");
    builtinParsE->setRStuff(ruleStuff);
    builtinParsE->groupBody->flags.noPrint = 1;

addString reaches addAttribute, and addAttribute is where hasTraits is written.
At that instant builtinParsE is not yet noPrint, so the inline test passes and
hasTraits is raised on a rule whose only attribute is decoration. The flag that
would repair it -- updateContentFlags, which rebuilds hasTraits with the same
test and gets the right answer because by then noPrint IS set -- has exactly
one caller in the tree, moveTo, and nothing on the parse path reaches it.

CANDIDATE: have setParse call field.updateContentFlags() after it has marked
both decoration attributes noPrint. One line, at Generate.rtn's setParse, after
the second noPrint assignment. hasAttributeS would keep reading TRUE, which is
correct -- the node IS marked up -- and hasTraits would fall back to 0 on a
rule whose attributes are all decoration.

WHAT WOULD PROMOTE IT: re-run ROW 3. The AFTER line must read hasAttributeS 1
hasTraitS 0. Then re-run the ROW 4 census with the gate on hasTraitS and
setParse ON: the setParse-RUNS column must move to 28 AND / 8 OR and StatemenT
must emit OR, while the hasAttributeS row of that column stays at 36 / 0.

WHAT WOULD KILL IT: ROW 3's AFTER line still reading hasTraitS 1, which would
mean a second writer is raising the flag somewhere addAttribute is not, or the
rebuild is not seeing the same children the gate does.

GRADE: BEST GUESS. The MECHANISM is measured -- the 2x2 and ROW 3 are not
inference. The REPAIR SHAPE has had no ruling and no build, so it is not RULED
and it is certainly not REMEDY. It is one line and it is Tony's to call, and it
touches setParse, which is on every rule's path.
